# PostgreSQL — the app's DB (Users/Sites/Scenarios/Suites/Tags/Runs/ScenarioResults), replacing
# Azure Table Storage (see specs/REQ-029_postgres_migration.md at the repo root). Local dev runs
# this via Docker: `npm run db:up` (or `npm run dev:all`, which brings it up automatically), then
# `npm run db:migrate` once to create the schema. Port 5435 (not the default 5432/5433/5434) because
# those are already taken by other local projects on this machine — see docker-compose.yml.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://smoke_test_runner:smoke_test_runner_dev@localhost:5435/smoke_test_runner?schema=public"

# Evidence images only now (the DB moved to Postgres above) — points at the Azurite emulator (run
# `npm run azurite` in another terminal, or `npm run dev:all`). This is Azurite's well-known,
# publicly-documented dev storage account key — NOT a secret, safe to keep in this example file.
AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING="DefaultEndpointsProtocol=http;AccountName=devstoreaccount1;AccountKey=Eby8vdM02xNOcqFlqUwJPLlmEtlCDXJ1OUzFT50uSRZ6IFsuFq2UVErCz4I6tq/K1SZFPTOtr/KBHBeksoGMGw==;TableEndpoint=http://127.0.0.1:10002/devstoreaccount1;BlobEndpoint=http://127.0.0.1:10000/devstoreaccount1;"

# Signs the login session JWT (see lib/auth/session.ts). This example value is
# fine for local dev only — generate a fresh one for production
# (`openssl rand -base64 32`) and set it as a real secret on whatever hosts
# the app; never commit a production value.
AUTH_SECRET="z6QIAvWcUpYdpiq2xEDjUBSRsJA5wZmGuyQr3ydaZWk="

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production: DATABASE_URL should point at a real hosted Postgres instance (provider not yet
# decided — see REQ-027's note in TODO.md, this is an open question for a future REQ, not resolved
# here). AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING for Evidence still needs a real Azure Storage Account:
#
#   AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING="DefaultEndpointsProtocol=https;AccountName=<storage-account-name>;AccountKey=<primary-key>;BlobEndpoint=https://<storage-account-name>.blob.core.windows.net/;"
#
# (Or, better than a raw connection string: use managed identity / Key Vault reference once
# provisioned, so no secret sits in an env var at all.)
